<?php
/** en strings of the theme's patterns (semantic keys). */
return array(
	'about_0_a' => 'Maren Holt and Kofi Mensah opened Teate Clinic in 2014 after they met working in a sports-club clinic. They wanted a quieter room of their own: two osteopaths, one pale couch, and enough time to listen before anyone is asked to lie down. Halbrook is home. The clinic sits on the ground floor of 14 Linden Row, step-free from the street, with light oak, white linen and window light.',
	'about_0_b' => 'The line they still use is the tagline on the card: Hands-on care for desks and sport. Maren spends her clinic days with the neck, shoulders and lower back of people who work at a desk. Kofi sees runners, cyclists and swimmers who train for fun and arrive with a knee, an ankle, a hip or a shoulder that will not settle. They share the week so that evening hours stay available mainly for people coming straight from work.',
	'about_0_h' => 'Opened in 2014',
	'about_1_a' => 'Treatment at Teate Clinic is done with the hands. A visit starts with what you tell us and with watching how you stand, sit, bend and walk. Only then do the hands work on the part that is stiff, guarded or simply tired from the way the week has gone. For desk workers that is often the neck, a headache at the end of the day, or a shoulder that sits up by late afternoon. It is also a lower back that has been in one chair too long.',
	'about_1_b' => 'For amateur athletes it is a niggle that showed up on a run, a ride or in the pool. The hands are slow on purpose. Maren or Kofi will say what they are doing and will leave you with two or three exercises you can do at home. We would rather spend ten minutes listening than guess. The room stays simple so the hour stays on you: pale walls, fresh linen, and the osteopath beside the couch.',
	'about_1_h' => 'Hands-only care',
	'about_2_a' => 'Teate Clinic is an osteopathy room, and the boundary around that work is plain. This is not a gym. There are no machines, no ultrasound and no needles. We do not diagnose disease, and we never tell anyone that a visit fixes a back, a neck or a knee. If something you tell us needs a doctor, we stop and refer you. That includes fever, numbness, night pain and a fall.',
	'about_2_b' => 'We will say so in the room and we will not carry on with hands-on treatment that belongs somewhere else. Many people need two to four follow-ups over a few weeks. Some need only one visit. We say so honestly, including when the honest advice is that you do not need to come back. After a visit you receive a receipt. Many private health plans repay part of a visit, and the receipt is yours to claim.',
	'about_2_h' => 'What we are not',
	'about_3_a' => 'The treatment room is one pale space on the ground floor. Each morning the low couch is made with fresh white linen. A wooden stool, a plant and a sheer curtain sit in the window light. Patients stay clothed, in a t-shirt, and in shorts when the visit is about a knee. Maren or Kofi works with the hands on the shoulder, the back or the limb that brought you in.',
	'about_3_b' => 'The close work is ordinary and quiet: two hands resting on a lower back over a pale t-shirt, or one hand supporting a seated shoulder while the other meets the shoulder blade. You can see the street trees if you look toward the window. People arrive from offices and from weekend training, and the room is meant to make the next step obvious. That next step is a conversation about where it hurts, then a careful look at how you move.',
	'about_3_h' => 'The room',
	'about_4_a' => 'A weekday at Teate Clinic follows the same ledger. At 07:45 the rooms are aired and the couches are made with fresh linen. The first patient is seen at 08:00. At 12:30 the notes are written and Inès Laurent returns calls from the front desk. Afternoon visits begin at 13:30. From 17:00 to 19:30 the evening slots are kept mainly for people coming from work.',
	'about_4_b' => 'At 19:45 the laundry goes on and the rooms are reset for the morning. The doors are open Monday to Friday from 08:00 to 19:30. Saturday runs from 08:30 to 13:00. Sunday and public holidays the clinic is closed. The last first-visit start is 18:30 on a weekday and 12:00 on Saturday. The phone desk is open Monday to Friday from 08:00 to 18:00, on +00 44 555 0172. Email hello@teate-clinic.example reaches the same desk.',
	'about_4_h' => 'A day in the clinic',
	'about_5_a' => 'Inès Laurent is at the front desk. She books every visit and answers the phone. If you write to hello@teate-clinic.example or call +00 44 555 0172, Inès is the person who reads the message and replies. She asks where it hurts, which days suit you, and whether this is a first visit, a follow-up, a short follow-up, a sport check or a workplace visit. She replies within one working day.',
	'about_5_b' => 'Ruth Adeyemi is the practice manager. She writes the clinic letter, the monthly note that carries one exercise, any change to the hours, and a few lines from Maren or Kofi. Inès and Ruth keep the book, the linen and the letter so the two osteopaths can stay with the person on the couch. You meet Inès when you arrive to fill in the one-page form. You meet Ruth\'s writing if you ask for the clinic letter.',
	'about_5_h' => 'Inès Laurent and Ruth Adeyemi',
