	'clin_more' => 'Meet the clinicians →',
	'clinicians_0_a' => 'Maren Holt is an osteopath and a co-founder of Teate Clinic. She works with the neck, the shoulders and the lower back of desk workers: stiffness, headaches at the end of the day, a shoulder that sits up, and backs that have been sat on, lifted with, or stirred up by moving house. Her days in the room are Monday, Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday. She trained as an osteopath, learning to listen, to watch how a person moves, and to treat with the hands.',
	'clinicians_0_b' => 'She met Kofi Mensah while they were both working in a sports-club clinic, and in 2014 they opened this practice together. In the room she is often the one supporting a seated shoulder, one hand under the joint and the other at the shoulder blade, asking what the afternoon at the desk has been like. Daniel Okafor and Tomás Rivera both saw her for a finite run of visits. She will tell you if one visit is enough, and she will tell you if a doctor should see you before she continues.',
	'clinicians_0_h' => 'Maren Holt',
	'clinicians_1_a' => 'Kofi Mensah is an osteopath and a co-founder of Teate Clinic. He works with sport niggles: knees, ankles, hips and shoulders for runners, cyclists and swimmers who train for fun. He runs and cycles himself, so a conversation about a long run or a commute by bike stays practical. His days are Monday, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday evenings, which is when many amateur athletes can get to Halbrook after work or after training.',
	'clinicians_1_b' => 'He trained as an osteopath in the same kind of hands-on way as Maren, and he met her in a sports-club clinic before they opened Teate Clinic in 2014. In a sport check he watches a slow forward bend or an easy stride before he treats, hands at his sides until the movement has shown him something. Sofia Brandt\'s knee is the kind of problem he sees: a weekend runner, a sport check, three follow-ups, one strength session a week, and an autumn half marathon run at an easy pace. He will say if you do not need another appointment.',
	'clinicians_1_h' => 'Kofi Mensah',
	'clinicians_2_a' => 'Maren Holt is in on Monday, Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday. She is the person to book for neck and shoulders from desk work, and for lower back from sitting, lifting or moving house. Kofi Mensah is in on Monday, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday evenings. He is the person to book for a sport niggle of the knee, ankle, hip or shoulder. On Monday and Thursday you can ask for either of them.',
	'clinicians_2_b' => 'Evening slots from 17:00 to 19:30 are kept mainly for people coming from work. Saturday is open from 08:30 to 13:00, with the last first visit at 12:00, and that morning is one of Maren\'s days. The clinic phone is +00 44 555 0172. The desk answers Monday to Friday, 08:00 to 18:00. If you have no preference, ask Inès Laurent for either clinician. The first hour is the same length whoever you see: 60 minutes at EUR 95, with the same 10, 15, 25 and 10 minute pace.',
	'clinicians_2_h' => 'Days and who to book',
	'clinicians_3_a' => 'Inès Laurent runs the front desk. She books every visit and answers the phone. A booking message to hello@teate-clinic.example comes to her, and she replies within one working day. She will ask where it hurts, which days suit you, and whether this is your first visit. Ruth Adeyemi is the practice manager. She writes the clinic letter: once a month, one exercise, any change to the opening hours, and a short note from Maren or Kofi.',
	'clinicians_3_b' => 'Inès and Ruth do not treat patients. If you need the couch, you are booking Maren Holt or Kofi Mensah. If you need a receipt, a change of time, or a workplace visit at your office, Inès is the person who arranges it. The workplace visit is 90 minutes at your office, desk set-ups for up to four people, at EUR 240. She confirms the day with you before anyone travels.',
	'clinicians_3_h' => 'Inès Laurent and Ruth Adeyemi',
	'clinicians_h1' => 'Clinicians',
	'clinicians_lede' => 'Two osteopaths treat at Teate Clinic. Maren Holt and Kofi Mensah founded the practice in 2014, and they still see every patient themselves.',
	'comp_back_h' => 'Lower back',
	'comp_back_p' => 'From long sitting, from lifting, from moving house, or from a sudden twinge. Tell us which of those it was. We would rather spend the opening minutes listening than guess. You stand, bend and walk before anyone treats. The work is hands only, on the low couch, with no machines in the room. The plan often includes how to lift and how to break up the sitting.',
	'comp_h' => 'Three things people come in with',
	'comp_l' => 'PROBLEM',
	'comp_more' => 'More about this →',
	'comp_neck_h' => 'Neck and shoulders from desk work',
	'comp_neck_p' => 'Stiffness through the afternoon, headaches at the end of the day, and a shoulder that sits up. The week has usually been a long one at a screen. We ask when it started and what makes it better or worse, then watch how you sit, stand and turn. Treatment is with the hands. Two or three exercises go home with you, small enough for a working day. Maren Holt sees most of these visits.',
	'comp_sport_h' => 'Sport niggles',
	'comp_sport_p' => 'Knees, ankles, hips and shoulders, for runners, cyclists and swimmers who train for fun. A weekend run, a regular ride, a swim before work. Kofi Mensah sees many of these visits, including on Friday evenings. A sport check looks at how you move for that sport and includes treatment. The aim is a clearer week of training, not a promise about a race.',
	'conditions_0_a' => 'Stiffness by the end of the day, headaches that arrive with the afternoon, and a shoulder that sits up toward the ear: this is what we hear most from people who work at a desk. The week has been spent looking slightly down, or off to one side, and the neck has been holding on. What we often do is the first hour in full. Ten minutes of talk, fifteen minutes watching you sit, stand and turn, then twenty-five minutes of hands-on work on the neck, the shoulder and the shoulder blade, and ten minutes for a plan.',
	'conditions_0_b' => 'Maren Holt sees most of these visits, on Monday, Tuesday, Thursday and Saturday. What you can do at home is usually small: two or three exercises, a change in screen height, and a reason to stand before the afternoon locks in. Daniel Okafor, an accountant, came after the year-end deadlines. Across four visits over six weeks he changed his screen height, learned two exercises, and could check over his shoulder on the bike again without turning his whole body. Your plan may be shorter. We will tell you.',
	'conditions_0_h' => 'Neck and shoulders from desk work',
	'conditions_1_a' => 'Lower back trouble here comes from long sitting, from lifting, from moving house, or from a sudden twinge that did not have a dramatic cause. What we often do is watch how you bend and how you come back up, then treat with the hands while you lie on the linen couch, face down, over a t-shirt. The hour still follows the same split: talk, movement, hands-on work and a plan. Maren Holt sees most of these visits.',
	'conditions_1_b' => 'What you can do at home is learn how to lift, and how to break up long sitting, so the back is not asked to hold one shape until evening. Tomás Rivera, a designer, did that work over three visits after he moved house. He now comes back twice a year for a service, which is his phrase, and his rhythm. Some people need two to four follow-ups over a few weeks. Some need one visit. If fever, numbness, night pain or a fall is part of what you tell us, we stop and refer you to a doctor.',
	'conditions_1_h' => 'Lower back',
	'conditions_2_a' => 'Knees, ankles, hips and shoulders, for runners, cyclists and swimmers who train for fun: that is the third complaint. This room is for the person who runs on weekends, rides to work or swims before the office. What we often do is a sport check, 50 minutes at EUR 85, with a movement review for running, cycling or swimming, and treatment in the same visit. Kofi Mensah leads this work. He runs and cycles himself, and he is in the clinic on Monday, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday evenings.',
	'conditions_2_b' => 'What you can do at home might be one strength session a week and a slower build in the long run. That is what Sofia Brandt used for the outside of her left knee. She had a sport check and three follow-ups, then ran her autumn half marathon at an easy pace. Shorts help if the niggle is at the knee, the ankle or the hip. Kofi will say if the training week should change, and he will say if you do not need another appointment.',
	'conditions_2_h' => 'Sport niggles',
	'conditions_3_a' => 'We do not diagnose disease. If something you tell us needs a doctor, we stop and refer you. Four things always pause the visit, and we keep them on the same ledger so they are easy to remember. Fever means we do not treat through an illness that belongs with a doctor. Numbness means we do not keep working on an area that has gone numb. Night pain, the kind that wakes you and stays, needs a medical opinion before osteopathy continues.',
	'conditions_3_b' => 'A fall means we want a doctor to see you before hands-on treatment. We would rather spend ten minutes listening than guess, and listening includes knowing when 14 Linden Row is the wrong room. Bring the one-page form even if you are unsure, because the talk at the start of the hour is where this comes out. Inès Laurent can help you change or cancel a later visit. Cancel up to 24 hours before at no cost, by phone or by email.',
	'conditions_3_h' => 'When we send you to a doctor',
	'conditions_h1' => 'Conditions',
	'conditions_lede' => 'People come to Teate Clinic with three kinds of trouble: neck and shoulders from desk work, a lower back, or a sport niggle from running, cycling or swimming.',
	'contact_0_a' => 'The address is 14 Linden Row, Halbrook. The clinic is on the ground floor, and the way in is step-free. Phone +00 44 555 0172. Email hello@teate-clinic.example. Open Monday to Friday, 08:00 to 19:30. Open Saturday, 08:30 to 13:00. Closed Sunday and public holidays. The phone desk is Monday to Friday, 08:00 to 18:00. The last first visit starts at 18:30 Monday to Friday and at 12:00 on Saturday.',
	'contact_0_b' => 'Evening slots from 17:00 to 19:30 are kept mainly for people coming from work. Inès Laurent answers the phone and the inbox, and she replies within one working day. Ruth Adeyemi writes the monthly clinic letter if you have asked to receive it. For a booking, say where it hurts and whether you want Maren Holt, Kofi Mensah, or either. The same details are on the booking page if you already know the visit type.',
	'contact_0_h' => 'Address, hours, phone and email',
	'contact_1_a' => 'Linden Row is a quiet, tree-lined street, and the clinic door is a ground-floor glass door in a pale grey-green frame. A bicycle rack stands by the door. If you come by bike, leave it on that rack. You can also come by bus and walk the last part from the stop. We do not publish a route number, because the useful stop depends on where you start. Parking is two streets away, so if you drive, leave time to walk in before your appointment.',
	'contact_1_b' => 'Arrive 5 minutes early when it is your first visit, so the one-page form is done before the hour starts. Saturday closes at 13:00, and the last first visit that day starts at 12:00. A weekday evening visit can start as late as 19:30 for a returning patient, while a first visit on a weekday starts by 18:30. The phone desk can talk you through the timing if you call before 18:00.',
	'contact_1_h' => 'Getting here',
	'contact_2_a' => 'The entrance is step-free. Treatment is on the ground floor, so the couch is on the same level as the street door. The couch is low, made with white linen, and you use it in the clothes you wore from work or from a run. Shorts are useful when we need to see a knee, an ankle or a hip. Inès Laurent is at the front desk when you arrive.',
	'contact_2_b' => 'If you want a little more time at the door, or you want to know how the room is arranged before you come, call +00 44 555 0172 on a weekday between 08:00 and 18:00, or email hello@teate-clinic.example. Say so when you book and she will note it. The pale room has a wooden stool and space beside the couch for the osteopath to stand and watch you move. That movement check is part of every first hour, and it happens in the same room as the treatment.',
	'contact_2_h' => 'Access',
	'contact_3_a' => 'The contact form asks for your name, your email, a message, and your agreement that Teate Clinic may contact you about that message. The button says Send message. You can also choose Email the clinic, which writes to hello@teate-clinic.example with the subject Message for Teate Clinic. Use this for questions about hours, about finding the door, or about whether the clinic is the right place for what you feel.',
	'contact_3_b' => 'For a booking, tell us where it hurts, which days suit you, and whether this is your first visit. Inès replies within one working day. We do not diagnose disease by email. If you already know about fever, numbness, night pain or a fall, see a doctor, and tell us so we can pause any visit on the book. Cancel up to 24 hours before at no cost. Hands only: no machines, no ultrasound, no needles.',
	'contact_3_h' => 'Writing to the clinic',
	'contact_email_button' => 'Email the clinic',
	'contact_fallback_note' => 'Write to us at hello@teate-clinic.example with your name, what you would like to ask and a way to reply. Inès Laurent answers within one working day.',
