	'ways_h' => 'Find your way',
	'ways_l' => 'PAGES',
	'why_1' => 'A visit at Teate Clinic is hands-on once treatment begins, and the room has been set up for that since 2014. There is no machine, no ultrasound and no needles. Maren Holt and Kofi Mensah work on a low couch at 14 Linden Row, with window light and fresh linen changed before the day starts. They use their hands to feel where a shoulder is holding on, where a back has stopped sharing the work, and where a knee is guarding a movement you still want to make. Talk comes first, then watching, then the hands, then a short plan. That order is the method.',
	'why_2' => 'Desk work and amateur sport need the same kind of attention, even though the week looks different. A neck that stiffens after a long sitting day and a knee that niggles after a weekend run are both stories about load, hour after hour. We would rather spend ten minutes listening than guess. You say where it hurts, when it started, and what makes it better or worse. Then we watch you stand, sit, bend and walk. The treatment that follows is for the person on the couch, not for a label on a form. Two or three exercises go home with you. They are small on purpose, so they can sit beside a working day or one strength session in a training week.',
	'why_3' => 'We are not a gym, and we do not diagnose disease. If something you tell us needs a doctor — fever, numbness, night pain, or a fall — we stop and refer you. Many people need two to four follow-ups over a few weeks. Some need only one visit, and we say so when that is what we see. We make no promise about an outcome, and we do not talk about a visit as a cure. What we can keep to is simpler: a clear look at how you move, hands-on care, and a plan you can actually repeat. Hands only. No machines, no ultrasound, no needles. If a private health plan repays part of a visit, we give you a receipt to claim.',
	'why_cap' => 'Hands only: no machines, no ultrasound, no needles.',
	'why_h' => 'Why hands, not machines',
